wel obstruction. Given the PJS in history, small bowel intussusception would be the most likely diagnosis. Small bowel obstruction\npresents with central colicky abdominal pain, early development of nausea and vomiting, mild to moderate abdominal distention, and constipation/ obstipation. As the condition\ndeteriorates, bowel ischemia, necrosis, perforation, and peritonitis can develop.\n(Option A) Although people with PJS are at increased risk of GI cancers at younger ages, cecal cancer is a less likely diagnosis in this scenario. Cecal cancer presents with fatigue\ndue to chronic blood loss rather than signs and symptoms of bowel obstruction.\n(Option B) Acute appendicitis can also present with periumbilical pain that gradually shifts and fixes in the right lower quadrant. Nausea and probably vomiting may follow the pain.\nThe patient usually has anorexia and regional tenderness and often rebound in the right lower quadrant. However, bowel obstruction, as seen in this patient, is not a feature.","source_answer_status":"present","current_answer":null,"current_answer_status":"needs_verification","current_evidence":[],"currentness_risk":"medium","currentness_keywords":["age"],"likely_visual_question":true,"parser_confidence":"high","parser_mode":"strict_ocr_tolerant","text_method":"native","parser_version":"2.7.1-parser-only-conservative-staging-2026-09-22","stem_hash":"f8be0c42fb44419eabeededc9c1a5dacea49d422","duplicate_group_id":"exact-f8be0c42fb44","duplicate_sources":[{"record_id":"1f02fbb5f7222892b094","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"Amedex 2023.pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Amedex 2023.pdf","source_page":587,"source_qid":null}]}',1,'v2.9.4','2026-09-24T00:00:00.000Z') ON CONFLICT(record_id) DO UPDATE SET source_file=excluded.source_file,source_page=excluded.source_page,domain=excluded.domain,stem=excluded.stem,options_json=excluded.options_json,source_answer=excluded.source_answer,source_explanation=excluded.source_explana